2026-08-24 — THE STREETS NO BLOCK IMPLIES.

`gen_geo2.py` ships classcode 0-4 and drops 5-6, and gives its reason in its own
header: "the gap between two real blocks already IS the street." That is true on
the ordinary grid and false wherever there are no blocks. The Hunters Point
Naval Shipyard is ONE assessor block, so nothing on the page stands for its
streets and they are simply absent — which is how the map came to draw buildings
with no streets under them.

It also had a second, quieter defect on the same line: `int(Ln["c"] or 5)`.
3psu-pn9h publishes classcode 0-6; 780 rows publish NONE, and `or 5` called
every one of them "local residential", which is the class this file drops. A
null class is not class 5. It is no class, and it now ships as 7.

THE NEW TEST, measured rather than assumed: a dropped centreline that no
published block EDGE comes within IMPLY_M (30 m) of, along at least IMPLY_FRAC
(a quarter) of its length. Edges are walked and not filled, because a street is
implied by a block edge facing it and the interior of a 3 km block implies
nothing about its own perimeter. It is run against the rings this same file is
about to ship, so the question is asked of exactly what the reader will see.

    846 lines · 3,082 vertices · 131.6 km · 29 KB raw, ~12 KB gzip
    every one carrying a published street name
    class 5: 554   class 7 (no published class): 280   class 6 (ramps): 12
    Presidio 478 · Bayview Hunters Point 113 · Treasure Island 110 ·
    Golden Gate Park 85 · Marina (Fort Mason) 11 · Lincoln Park 10
    632 of them on a federal layer

EACH LINE CARRIES ITS OWN `cnn`, recovered here and PROVED, not joined by
position. geo/build_streets.py drops cnn when it writes streetlines.json, so the
same filter is replayed over the fetched dataset and the join is proved the way
encode_city_chunks.py::_centrelines() proves its own: Douglas-Peucker keeps a
line's FIRST vertex, so that vertex survives the 3 m simplify untouched and is
an exact witness. Measured over all 16,676 non-paper centrelines: worst
first-vertex disagreement 0.0000 m. A single mismatch raises.

IT IS A SEPARATE ARRAY, `G.streetsUnimplied`, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
`TIME.street.yr` and street-data.js's `cov` mask are each keyed one-to-one on
`G.streets`, and encode_city_chunks.py's `_centrelines()` replays a filter to
recover `cnn` positionally. Adding 846 entries to `streets` would silently shift
all four. Verified after the change: the `streets` object is BYTE-IDENTICAL, and
`_centrelines()` still returns n=4,895 with worstJoin 0.000195 m.

NOTE ON gen/geo-data.js. It was SPLICED, not regenerated: the new key was
inserted after the `streets` object and every other byte left alone. A plain
re-run of gen_geo2.py is not byte-reproducible today — `blocks.vx`/`vy` come
back with 2,079 of 74,042 deltas moved by exactly 1 quantum, on the unmodified
file as well as this one. That wobble is sub-decimetre and invisible, but it is
a determinism defect the project's own rule forbids, it is NOT caused by this
change, and it should be fixed before anyone regenerates geo-data.js in a shared
tree. See DATA-TRUTH.md section 8.

